The Problem: Managing Unsafe C Pointers in RustAssume we have a proprietary C library for parsing custom network packets. The C header () looks like this:typedef struct Packet {
    int id;
    char* payload;
} Packet;

// Allocates and parses a packet. Returns NULL on failure.
Packet* parse_packet(const uint8_t* data, size_t len);

// Must be called to prevent memory leaks.
void free_packet(Packet* pkt);
If we call these directly, we burden the Rust user with manual memory management, entirely defeating the purpose of using Rust.The Safe Rust Wrapper SolutionWe use the Drop trait to ensure memory is always freed, and lifetimes to ensure the payload isn't accessed after it's freed.use std::ffi::CStr;
use std::os::raw::{c_char, c_int, c_uchar};

// Bindings typically generated by bindgen
#[repr(C)]
struct CPacket {
    id: c_int,
    payload: *mut c_char,
}

extern "C" {
    fn parse_packet(data: *const c_uchar, len: usize) -> *mut CPacket;
    fn free_packet(pkt: *mut CPacket);
}

// Our Safe Abstraction
pub struct Packet {
    inner: *mut CPacket,
}

impl Packet {
    pub fn parse(data: &[u8]) -> Result {
        // SAFETY: We pass a valid pointer and length. 
        // We check the return value for NULL.
        let ptr = unsafe { parse_packet(data.as_ptr(), data.len()) };
        if ptr.is_null() {
            Err("Failed to parse packet")
        } else {
            Ok(Packet { inner: ptr })
        }
    }

    pub fn id(&self) -> i32 {
        // SAFETY: inner is guaranteed to be non-null and valid.
        unsafe { (*self.inner).id }
    }

    pub fn payload(&self) -> &str {
        // SAFETY: C strings must be null-terminated. We tie the lifetime
        // of the returned &str to `&self`, ensuring it doesn't outlive the struct.
        unsafe {
            let c_str = CStr::from_ptr((*self.inner).payload);
            c_str.to_str().unwrap_or("Invalid UTF-8")
        }
    }
}

// Implement Drop to automatically free the C memory
impl Drop for Packet {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        // SAFETY: The pointer is valid and this is only called once.
        unsafe {
            free_packet(self.inner);
        }
    }
}
Code Annotations & Safety Guarantees Crucial for ensuring the Rust struct has the exact memory layout as the C struct. Without this, Rust might reorder fields, leading to memory corruption. vs : FFI boundaries deal in raw pointers (). We encapsulate the raw pointer inside a safe struct (). Converts a C-style null-terminated string into a Rust string slice. The lifetime of the returned  is implicitly tied to  through lifetime elision, preventing use-after-free bugs. This is the magic. When a  goes out of scope, Rust automatically calls , invoking the C  function. The consumer of our library literally cannot cause a memory leak.By carefully building these safe boundaries around unsafe C code, DevOps teams can migrate critical infrastructure to Rust incrementally, retaining legacy business logic while eliminating entirely classes of memory bugs.

We run a B2B platform for storing, processing, protecting, and delivering video over our own CDN, for customers from small startups to large enterprises. Under the hood that's high-load services, hundreds of thousands of requests per second, terabits of traffic, and a stack of home-grown components in Go, including our own CDN and DNS.This story is about one hard problem inside that stack — TLS delivery: how our team took it from a stubborn ceiling to 70+ Gbps per 1U server, why HTTPS is more than a checkbox on a security list, and what we learned about kernel TLS, zero-copy, and a certificate default that quietly cost us seconds per handshake.Kinescope (disclosure: I'm a co-founder of Kinescope. The Gbps are real, the bias is optional) is a SaaS product, so it runs over the internet, and like almost every modern service that means everything is HTTPS. That's the de facto standard now.The flip side is that HTTPS isn't free: setting up TLS (the handshake, the key exchange, the encryption itself) costs time and CPU that plain HTTP never pays. At our scale, "a little more expensive" turns into real hardware.A quick reminder of the shape of the problem: HTTPS is just HTTP over TLS. The HTTP protocol is unchanged, and TLS encrypts the transport underneath it. Simplified, TLS works like this:A normal TCP connection is established.A TLS handshake runs: client and server agree on encryption parameters — they pick a cipher (AES, ChaCha20), exchange keys, and confirm both sides support the choice.After the handshake, data flows encrypted over that secure connection.To send anything, data is gathered, encrypted, and transmitted; on the way back it's received and decrypted. That's simple on paper, but the cost hides in the details of each step.Is encryption really expensive?You may hear a lot that encryption is expensive. When I was digging into this, I found the usual reassurance that Google moved to encryption-everywhere back in 2010 with no measurable performance hit, though nobody says at what load. Stack Overflow answers tend to put it at 1–2% CPU, 1–6% network, and a small bump in latency. One CDN vendor even published a neat table with precise figures.Both the Stack Overflow numbers and that table are close to useless. They measure someone else's workload on someone else's hardware. The only figure that matters is the one you measure on your own traffic, and ours, as you'll see, landed nowhere near 1–2%.A weekend the racks nearly tripped their breakersWe added support for ChaCha20-Poly1305, which is lighter on the CPU and matters for older, low-powered Android phones that struggle with hardware AES. For those devices, ChaCha is simply easier.One quiet Saturday, an alert fired: CPU consumption was climbing. Right behind it came an alert from the data center — the racks were near their power limit and at risk of being cut. Then customers started writing in: "everything's slow".Our first guess was the happy one: “success, just a traffic surge”. Except traffic was  than usual. What had really happened: adding ChaCha broke our detection of hardware AES support, so we started handing ChaCha to  client, including the ones with AES acceleration. That ate all the CPU.Step one was knowing whether we even had the problem, so we added metrics. The interesting part: traffic from the same servers but different customers showed wildly different device profiles, and live traffic showed its own correlation. The lesson is to check against your own data, as device mix varies enormously.In a genuine resource crunch (a high share of Android ChaCha requests and pegged CPUs), there's a blunt technical compromise: temporarily lower the TLS version and drop ChaCha support. It's not great for security or compatibility, but in a crisis it can stabilize things. The device runs hotter and burns more battery and CPU, while your servers absorb two to three times the traffic because the encryption load drops.Encrypting large volumes of data is always costly, and the CPU is only part of it. Sometimes it's so heavy it reaches down into other layers. The team at Netflix has said this is one of their hardest, most interesting problems. They write their own operating system purely to serve files from disk efficiently, and in testing they hit up to 800 GB/s. We stop short of writing our own OS, but the lesson is the same: at this volume, every copy you can avoid is CPU and memory you get back.Our CDN, and where zero-copy breaksWe run our own CDN. At some point we dropped NGINX and wrote our own edge server in Go, which we call EDGE. Early on, TLS performance was a real problem, it couldn't keep up. We found: known, but at the time nobody was working on it. As a stopgap we put Hitch in front of EDGE as a TLS terminator, and that stabilized things.EDGE can serve data from local disk, serve data from another EDGE, and, with a proxy spreading traffic evenly, shard data and push it straight from network to network.For moving bytes we use the standard io.Copy, which every Go developer knows. Internally it leans on zero-copy, which avoids extra allocations and roughly halves the copying — a real win when you have a garbage collector to feed.func Copy(dst Writer, src Reader, buf []byte) (written int64, err error) { 
        // Similarly, if the writer has a ReadFrom method, use it to do the copy.
        if rf, ok = dst. (ReaderFrom); ok {
                return rf.ReadFrom(src)       
        }
        for {
                nr, er := src.Read(buf)
                if nr > 0 {
                        nw, ew := dst.Write(buf[0:nr])
                }    
       }
       return written, err
}
The ReadFrom interface is what we care about, because we live on the network. A TCP connection implements it, which lets us move data with fewer allocations:func (c *TCPConn) ReadFrom(r io.Reader) (int64, error) {
        if n, err, handled := spliceFrom(c.fd, r); handled {
                return n, err
        }
        if n, err, handled := sendFile(c.fd, r); handled {
                return n, err
        }  
        return genericReadFrom(c, r)
}
There are two calls here: spliceFrom, used when we write from network to network, and sendFile.In an operating system there are always two worlds: kernel space and user space. To read a file and push it to the network, data normally travels through memory, crossing between those two spaces. RAM is involved, the CPU helps, and it can look fast, but the crossing between spaces is often the real bottleneck. So system calls appeared that let you hand the kernel two descriptors (a source and a sink) and say "move this directly." The data never rises into user space, which is faster and leaner.TLS breaks that: the payload has to be encrypted first, and that happens in user space, so you can't hand the bytes over "as is."Our traffic is stable week to week, with clear seasonality. The exception is e-commerce sales events (some of our customers are large online stores), when load spikes hard enough that the network cards on some servers hit their throughput ceiling.Live streaming adds its own sharp spikes: traffic climbs fast, drops just as fast, then climbs again, and for most of the time a server stays mostly idle rather than using all its resources.We also went through a shift from VOD to live. At first we served live traffic through the ordinary EDGE servers, and it was inefficient, so we wrote a separate piece of software and ran it on its own machines, which worked noticeably better.That move came with its own demands on the hardware.We chase maximum throughput per 1U server, because that's the hardware we run — compact and dense. The targets:Working load: up to 70 Gbps.Headroom: up to 80 Gbps, so we don't run into the ceiling.The requirements come from the server because of that 1U configuration:CPU: Xeon Platinum 8176M @ 2.10 GHz, 56 coresRAM layout: 8 × 32 GB DDR4 (~23 Gbps per channel)Do the memory-bandwidth math against an 80 Gbps target and, in theory, the server offers around 160 Gbps. But once you copy data through user space, you're effectively left with about 80 Gbps, and you're already against the wall. So there's nothing to do but optimize.That same  discusses people serving around 145 Gbps over ordinary TLS, which is genuinely good, higher than ours. The catch is they run AMD EPYC, and one of those CPUs costs about what fifteen of our 1U servers do. At a comparable traffic output, our configuration is far cheaper.We traced the bottleneck to the link between Hitch (the TLS terminator) and our own service:Network stack: 23%, including loopback trafficWhen we're stuck, we bring in people who can look deeper into an app than we can, in our case the Tempesta team, who we work with regularly. Debugging together, we found that most of the CPU went to cache misses, and 60% of  came from copying data inside Hitch. That was the culprit, and it had to go.A couple of years earlier, before we served live traffic, we'd taken a first run at this in Rust.It worked. But supporting a second language is expensive: the whole team is on Go, and adding Rust meant more process, more training, more maintenance. We dropped the idea and felt relieved. Rust is a fine language, but carrying two stacks in one team just isn't worth it for us.TLS + zero-copy: move the encryption into the kernelWe had another idea. Zero-copy is efficient but only works on raw bytes, and TLS needs an encrypted payload you can't just hand off.  what if we push the encryption  to get around that?Frankly, the idea isn't ours, and it isn't new. We run FreeBSD in production, so this lineage is more than academic to us. Oracle Solaris had, which handled SSL traffic in the kernel to avoid context switches and talk directly to the crypto framework.What's telling is that at least three of the patch authors were from Mellanox, the high-performance NIC maker later bought by NVIDIA. The point was to push crypto past the kernel and onto the network card itself. That's how Netflix serves at 800 Gbps: encryption and transfer happen with minimal CPU involvement. Since Linux 6.0 you can also use zero-copy for read-only files, avoiding the user/kernel copy entirely when serving static content.The kTLS patch was merged into the kernel. Almost immediately, Filippo Valsorda (Go's TLS maintainer) wrote a proof of concept and a post,, which is still the de facto documentation for anyone touching kTLS; nothing clearer has appeared since.kTLS moves TLS encryption from user space into the Linux kernel; the is the reference. Almost all of it comes down to this sequence:Open an ordinary TCP connection, everything works as with normal TLS.Run the handshake in user space, with any library (OpenSSL, BoringSSL, and so on).Create a kTLS socket, much like setting SO_REUSEADDR.Pass the kernel, via setsockopt(), the encryption keys (key + IV) and TLS session parameters (such as the record sequence number).After that the socket behaves like a normal one — data is read and written already decrypted. write(), sendfile(), and splice() work transparently; read() is trickier and needs extra logic.We needed only outbound traffic, and support for TLS 1.2 and 1.3. Rather than lift examples from our production library (too much surrounding machinery), we took the latest Go, made a minimal patch, and built a clean example on top of it.The nice part is that Go's library already has almost everything, so we wrote very little.The first job is to grab the encryption keys. Go exposes them through two methods: setTrafficSecret() for TLS 1.3 and prepareCipherSpec() for TLS 1.2. We save the keys in the halfConn struct, where they're easy to work with and to hand to the kernel later through setsockopt().With the keys stored, we enable TLS on the socket as part of the handshake.Enabling it means checking which cipher the connection should use and passing the kernel that cipher spec as a raw set of bytes, then writing to the socket to declare that its upper-layer protocol is TLS.At this point kernel TLS is on but still does nothing, because the application is writing plaintext the old way. So we patch the write path: instead of the usual user-space handling, we write straight to the socket with write(). No wrapper: connect, pass the data, and the kernel encrypts it.func (c *Conn) writeRecordLocked(typ recordType, data []byte) (int, error) {
        if _, ok := c.out.cipher.(KTLSCipher); ok {
                switch typ {
                case recordTypeAlert:
                        return ktlsSendCtrlMessage(c.conn.(*net.TCPConn), typ, data)
                        // Я не нашел, где это может быть вызвано,
                        // но в доке KTLS оно описано как возможный случай
                case recordTypeHandshake, recordTypeChangeCipherSpec:
                        return ktlsSendCtrlMessage(c.conn.(*net.TCPConn), typ, data)
                case recordTypeApplicationData:
                        fmt.Println("KTLS write", len(data))
                        return c.write(data)
                default:
                        panic("KTLS: tried to send unsupported data type")
                }
         }
}
The data now goes out encrypted, but a plain write() still leaves performance on the table. Since we're on Go, we can use zero-copy to skip the extra allocations and the user-space copy. To thread it through the whole chain, we implement the ReadFrom interface on our own connection type, so data moves straight from the source (a file, say) into the socket with no intermediate copy.On production, a trace shows splice in the call path, which means data flows directly through the kernel while our application does nothing extra.To check it end to end, we ran a plain Go HTTP server with a file handler and hit it from a browser. It behaved perfectly: write() for the headers and sendfile() for the body over zero-copy, which keeps HTTP correct while pushing the transfer at full speed.On production we reached 73 Gbps, which was enough for us, with a server holding a little over 40,000 connections.There was a small degradation, but well inside our SLA and target service level.Look inside and the service is barely doing anything. The extra copying is gone. About 9% of CPU goes to AES-NI encrypt/decrypt (_encrypt_by_8), the main load, and the edge process itself is almost invisible.Yes, 9% is more than the 1–2% the internet promises, but it fits our model. On another server pushing around 40 Gbps, user-space CPU (where our app runs) showed just 1.4%.Writing our own library (really a small patch to the existing one) gave us a bonus: we could embed metrics. We added monitoring of ciphers, handshakes, and the rest — and that immediately surfaced something ugly. The TLS handshake was taking an indecent amount of time.We wanted to know why the handshake was so slow. The obvious move (push even more TLS logic into the kernel, since we already use kTLS) turns out not to be simple. Implementations and patches exist, but they aren't accepted into mainline Linux, for security reasons: network developers want to extend the kernel, security people push back because it widens the attack surface and complicates audits. You  use these in experimental or specialized setups, though:One aside: if you serve a lot of traffic and someone tells you "NGINX has QUIC now, it'll all just work," that's not quite true. QUIC by itself doesn't solve the copy-and-throughput problem. You either buy a very expensive machine or go deep with low-level solutions like these.Kernelizing the handshake was a dead end, so we kept digging into why ours was slow, and the cause turned out to be mundane. Like many teams, we started on paid certificates, then simplified by moving to automatic renewal through Let's Encrypt. It all seemed fine. But once we were deep in TLS debugging (trading code snippets in a shared chat), someone dropped in a finding that lit up the real cause of the slowness.A cryptography specialist on the Tempesta side glanced at our code and said, "You've got RSA in there." That's when it clicked: while switching to Let's Encrypt, we hadn't noticed that some certificates used RSA instead of ECDSA — and that was dragging down handshake time.Let's Encrypt issues RSA certificates by default. RSA is slow, especially under load; across implementations (GnuTLS, OpenSSL, WolfSSL, mbed TLS, Go) elliptic-curve algorithms are noticeably faster, and in Go the RSA gap is even wider, as the themselves. The community knows about it but doesn't push hard to fix it, because the fix is trivial: swap RSA certificates for ECDSA. We did, and saw the difference immediately.The same signing call, RSA versus ECDSA:RSA:   1.61s   sig, err := hs.cert.PrivateKey.(crypto.Signer).Sign(...)ECDSA:  40ms   sig, err := hs.cert.PrivateKey.(crypto.Signer).Sign(...)The signature went from 1.6 seconds to 40 milliseconds. Overall handshake time dropped about fourfold — the rest is a network round trip, which is slow but not something crypto can fix. On a benchmark with two certificates on the same VM (), RSA did 423 handshakes per second and elliptic curves did 833.Your numbers will differ with your hardware, but the order holds: switching the certificate type is a one-line change that pays off at every handshake.Session resumption, and the many-servers problemEven after ECDSA, handshakes were still expensive, because each new one needs a full cryptographic exchange. The long-known answer is session resumption (session tickets and friends), and Cloudflare has good write-ups on how much it cuts load. Better still, Go does it out of the box: the handshake implementation resumes TLS sessions automatically, and by default Go generates a fresh random key for session resumption every 24 hours, balancing security and efficiency with no manual tuning.Many servers, one session keySession resumption works well until you have many servers, which we do. Our infrastructure means the same domain (and, with anycast, the same IP) can be served by different machines, so requests from one client land on different servers, and each server has its own session context. That breaks the resumption.You might point out that persistent connections solve this, and they partly do: at 1,000 RPS the number of new handshakes drops noticeably thanks to keep-alive.But it doesn't fully fix short-lived or frequently reconnecting clients. And filter by HTTP 400-and-up and the picture changes: modern browsers, when a server returns 4xx or higher, close the connection and open a new one, so the number of handshakes spikes. For us that's routine — we serve video, and 4xx/5xx are a normal part of manifest handling (thank you, Apple). At small streams (up to 1,000 viewers) it's fine; at a major event with 100,000–150,000 viewers the system visibly sags.The fix was simple and we've run it for about a year: on an interval, we synchronize one shared session-ticket key across all servers.An alternative is to hardcode the session key as a constant, which is what we do on our CDN — not ideal for security, but acceptable here because we don't transmit sensitive data and we serve static, public content that's available to everyone anyway.For us HTTPS is less about secrecy and more about meeting modern requirements, mainly from browsers and devices. Serving public video, we'd happily skip the overhead if the ecosystem allowed it, but it doesn't. DRM content is a separate story, with its own encryption unrelated to TLS.A proxy that shards by URL hashNot all of our traffic goes straight through EDGE; some runs through a proxy, and that's where one of our most demanding customers lives: an online-school platform on our CDN. Its profile is awkward: a cache hit rate around 70%, 15–40 Gbps of traffic, billions of objects, and hundreds of zones. Distribute that purely by DNS and the load smears unevenly across machines, which hammers disks and servers. So we wrote our own proxy in Go that spreads traffic by a hash of the URL. It's crude, but it evens out the I/O when there are that many objects. Then we wired kTLS into the proxy too, and it got leaner still.That design proved itself the hard way. During one load of about 30–35 Gbps, an engineer's config mistake routed all of it to a single machine. That box ended up pushing 40 Gbps at 600% CPU (six cores) and held, and nothing fell over. With the old external TLS terminator in the path, it would have gone down.We got the result we needed: 70+ Gbps of encrypted video per 1U server, on hardware a fraction the cost of the EPYC alternative.We simplified the stack. Moving TLS into the application lets us drop the external terminator. Every extra module adds technical debt (harder to debug, update, and monitor), and removing Hitch made the infrastructure noticeably easier to run.It's more resilient. As the misroute above showed, kTLS in the proxy let one machine absorb 40 Gbps at 600% CPU without falling over, where the old external terminator would not have.We kept the native Go interface. Go's standard-library interfaces are excellent, built well by experienced people. When something's missing you write it: for kTLS we copied the part we needed, deleted the excess, added what was missing, and fixed the imports, keeping Go's own crypto at the core. It's a construction kit.kTLS in Go is on the way. After years in limbo, the proposal () is now accepted and tracked for implementation: transparent kTLS behind an opt-in GODEBUG=tlskernel=1, off by default, with the handshake still running in Go and only the socket read/write moved into the kernel. The write and sendfile path is the solid one; the read (RX) direction still has open issues upstream, which matches our own experience. We added reads once for TLS 1.3, but the Ubuntu 22.04 HWE kernel hangs the connection after five requests, so at our next TLS update we dropped everything read-related and kept only file sending.
